Nothing uses this. Same thing, bring it with the PR that needs it. If the       
cursors protocol lands in a different shape this is dead code nobody remembers  
to delete.                                                                      

src/terminal/stream.zig (confidence: 0.85)                                      

▌  •                                                                          
▌                    ':', ';' => {                                            
▌                                                                             
▌  •                                                                          
▌                        params.push(acc, c == ':');                          
▌                                                                             

This used to be an inline array store and a bit set. Now its a call with 4 or 5 
branches per ; in the hottest loop we have. You inlined digit for exactly this  
reason so I'm surprised push isn't. Did you measure it? I'd want bench numbers  
before/after, and probably an inline fast path for the len < INLINE_PARAMS case 
with the spill as a noinline cold call.                                         

src/terminal/Parser.zig (confidence: 0.70)                                      

▌  • pub fn push(self: *Params, value: u16, colon: bool) void {               
▌  •                                                                          
▌        if (self.failed) return;                                             
▌                                                                             
▌  •                                                                          
▌        if (self.len >= MAX_PARAMS) {                                        
▌                                                                             

Same comment as the stream side. The spill branch is fine being big but it      
shouldn't be in the same function as the inline store. Split the cold half out  
so the common path stays small.                                                 

src/terminal/Parser.zig (confidence: 0.55)                                      

▌ +/// Kakoune turned up with an SGR command that uses 17, then 24 on +///    
▌ empirical data, and is now 256 to match kitty's own limit, which the +///   
▌ cursors protocol is written against.                                        

Bikeshed, this reads like a changelog. The actual reason 256 is bounded at all  
is the bit set needs a static size, say that and drop the history. Also don't   
reference the cursors protocol until we have it.                                
